  "lineage": "Complete separately issued2:26 Dikoboda sombe (solo), Smithsonian UNES08054_103, recorded by Simha Arom in the Central African Republic in1971 and released1973. Publisher credits one young Aka woman singing; the collector describes this first performance as a solo melody, followed by a separately indexed duet. The chosen whole solo excludes that distinct duet and the drums, iron blades, whistles, clapping and ritual choruses documented for other tracks. No personal name invented; the archival label does not provide one. Authored unprocessed solo-voice rendition retaining the source’s intimate single melodic line. Broad improvOnFrame is an editorial melodic-elaboration realization within the collector’s documented modal and metric organization, not a claim to jazz harmony or a fixed transcribed tune. No piano/chordal backing added. All17 original voice controls explicitly resolved: sole unaccompanied singer and single layer; neutral effort/tract are editorial, while exact register, laryngeal mechanism, timbre, training, microtones and tuning remain unspecified. General Aka yodel descriptions are not silently assigned as a measured mechanism for this individual track. Controlled live room is a proposed new recording, not the1971 setting; no global temperament or capture hardware inferred.",
